[{:db/id "q-115880/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "60e07d00-ce67-5a79-852c-f5183f21fe6c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115880/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "c73878a6-febf-52be-b384-bb72d3c685e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115880/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "8caa5386-a39e-541b-81af-d68ba86a614f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115880/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "753cdb5a-4ee8-5337-a4d2-d4d47de5ca33" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115880/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "e1fa176e-e7b1-54ef-8d20-58f23d7c2046" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115880/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "85f09089-2328-5171-87e2-062d32b0c0e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115880/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-115880/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-115880/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-115880/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-115880/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-115880/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115880" :question/id #uuid "e4f07403-165c-563e-b91e-76206e676d8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115880" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/bc5b29fca0521e3da39993d0359e857333611c68c35e49c037ca7af031288ba3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y+4)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y+4)$\n- ${y}^{′}=-y(y+1)(y+4)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/cd73bc9f35e167537996a3b33b84471dcb8fefe2eccb4afd21ca55a23e6478fd.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y+4)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y+4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-y(y+1)(y+4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=-4,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=-1,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115880/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "954f8004-35b4-58ec-be26-2beccfd5f8e4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115880"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115888/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "15e7d479-5bee-5068-a8cd-0a5f6cbc2d3d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115888/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "7cf147bd-8efb-5e25-884a-946ae2acbd6c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115888/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "6ba4600f-9dec-53cc-a808-ea0bc1a9d7c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115888/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "28f361e7-2329-5da8-af60-0e78cc1948f5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115888/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "8effa258-b99d-5eea-8e6f-5c67e1051d16" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115888/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a831e90e-bf4c-5bdf-b54a-8af6adc87a44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115888/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-115888/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-115888/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-115888/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-115888/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-115888/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115888" :question/id #uuid "81165700-39d9-5e62-91be-1dd6097a0758" :question/math-academy-id "q-115888" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/2e2d4b78d8181efc77f65af0c3dc4b3110d0203e650006a0e99d9a8ba933b53f.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-(y-1)(y-4)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-1)(y-4)$\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y-1)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/7e207bc990a9dfbafa8474ad13c17482e21c323833be848f4ebbaa580abeb201.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=1$ and $y=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-(y-1)(y-4)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-1)(y-4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=0,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115888/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "954f8004-35b4-58ec-be26-2beccfd5f8e4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115888"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120109/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "a5df8253-1026-5c10-b261-19769cc09a41" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120109/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "4dd60902-55f2-591f-92ac-5f908e97f2fb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120109/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "fad63b7e-b8ff-59e9-85d5-0f4a555fb39b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120109/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "6639ee31-b828-535b-a581-8fbe92fc2a64" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120109/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "78119c6e-9f30-5aa2-b51e-bae7da5d1f7d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120109/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4004645-54fc-5341-a8f8-727e46b37ac2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120109/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-120109/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-120109/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-120109/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-120109/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-120109/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120109" :question/id #uuid "85f05352-e7af-5f82-b902-ef96f4b2f493" :question/math-academy-id "q-120109" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/3d24906da8cd181d40dbaec0095b8d51481babc152609fc378b03c61187220c3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)(y-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(2-y)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/7f8a042b9944b2cdf0e864b7dca760f094f4413f197befa05d6e5cbefb3841ba.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)(y-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=1,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=\\pm 1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(2-y)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120109/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "954f8004-35b4-58ec-be26-2beccfd5f8e4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120109"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120106/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "94c185cc-641f-5c64-9e4b-865f58373ec0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120106/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "dc2fee5c-e173-564f-a1ed-006c4ab030eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120106/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "302e782b-387a-56e5-9141-4ebb721233e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120106/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "81024ebc-ba12-5903-a09f-3c9e98cbd8fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120106/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "69e74a01-75a3-5cc9-bf84-43b063acb258" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120106/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae1911b-7e1d-564c-96da-b734d45bfb63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120106/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-120106/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-120106/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-120106/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-120106/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-120106/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120106" :question/id #uuid "bbf5d076-ae90-5952-a22b-48de744a1f0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-120106" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/76a0135eba2c33a3e69f9893d7c32a4cdc93e6d99825bbf6f72fb1db72b2a590.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=(y-a)(y-b)^{2}.$ Find the value of $ab.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/68f424470a3a8ff98d7f738ce207c240795185e48ee527a434d04282d9023ed7.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=1$ and $y=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y-1)(y-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1,b=4,$ and $ab=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-120106/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "954f8004-35b4-58ec-be26-2beccfd5f8e4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120106"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115876/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "dca62aa0-84de-553f-a9db-dab5f8677a20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115876/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "a8e6f230-2c29-5579-86d3-60ce540b7465" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115876/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "a830f33f-40f9-5a62-9912-1ef005ae4ef8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115876/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "feccdb42-0fe5-5c9c-a4a2-6760c6afbef8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115876/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "3ba2c7d0-a506-5798-adc3-71fdb7d8e1a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115876/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d41ad310-ab0d-515c-8afc-c90bf4ea5370" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115876/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer-field/choices ["q-115876/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-115876/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-115876/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-115876/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-115876/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115876" :question/id #uuid "7474d0ef-0afb-5a88-8581-bff8157f9b7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-115876" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/bedcab6fac21c2c178604e94f04f31bf1f423562fd653fd18ae5e0ea949e38dc.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=(y-a)(y-b).$ Find the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/84b459d5682327d618116cd5d3ef331263548c754d2d8e0759e6fcb084fd466c.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=-4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+2)(y+4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-2,b=-4,$ and $a+b=-6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-115876/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "954f8004-35b4-58ec-be26-2beccfd5f8e4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115876"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120147/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "fa8a3618-c166-5d0d-a93e-60a1fc372873" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120147/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "ba43216d-5e4f-5b1a-ade8-53db1877cebd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120147/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "f77e5a48-7719-5664-a340-f3a7143e5697" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120147/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "c1b5720d-ff97-5461-9c63-37e9e90d56d8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120147/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "e4528608-b8f6-52b6-a035-7eca4919a718" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120147/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a745b38-cfed-5b6d-87e0-481970eae405" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120147/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-120147/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-120147/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-120147/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-120147/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-120147/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120147" :question/id #uuid "f68947f6-727a-5aaa-a57d-2f5ec5b7262d" :question/math-academy-id "q-120147" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/d27af23ca398eba62c2ab1fe2ee2cf9bfdf736c16fdd8408d2a13e0d34dae32c.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-2)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y+2)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/ab076b61b443e031455f07c1e976467cc3b74651bfb5fc2fb39f89a74e156681.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-2)^{2}$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y+2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-2$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-120147/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ca086fd-9732-5c4b-9cb0-168433d79203"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120147"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-115466"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/b7feb77818b19ab8b61471df9057a15fb3c39d6babc4aeee56a73e1d3c3756cb.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/1e37d101f131279fc198fd97f03e6a35e15bc2d62622e1730aadbc316dc8d0bf.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}(y+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-115935/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "b7edd58f-d5f6-53ff-a7a8-54cdf7956e07" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115935/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "5270eac7-d57e-54a0-a8b5-cdc517b67383" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115935/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "b74286d9-ef23-5923-9e17-115ebf9afdf2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115935/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "40807088-e812-53d8-9ccc-c815156e772d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115935/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "0904ecf8-e274-57e8-a6ed-ef842d7fced8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115935/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f94163e5-5a0e-5e62-b4b0-4414a42a2e64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115935/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-115935/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-115935/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-115935/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-115935/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-115935/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115935" :question/id #uuid "af0eae53-a90a-516b-8984-504779f551ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-115935" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/57ecd122ba41b296b3884ecd4f748f6b4ac4bd1d26f14d7b5166c5fe6e33674b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y-1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(1-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/e109d237419aaba5efcc0c65c2d97c8f8ded17d18f98bb0a95eba3336d224590.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=\\pm 1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=\\pm 1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<1$ | $y>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=\\pm 1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<1$ | $y>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(1-y)$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-115935/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ca086fd-9732-5c4b-9cb0-168433d79203"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115935"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115941/selection/4a08887e14d194718cb72797be31ec63233d4df5b56923b20aae560d0fa5a147" :answer/id #uuid "c0807fc1-d9f6-50cb-bb30-e9bf902a2a61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115941/selection/c2aed1dd8c352973477ebb4eeeee8a60a835bc6afc8f05f5a6b1471f9ecded02" :answer/id #uuid "24472044-ee31-5574-8a3d-7ce5819a1884" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115941/selection/6c81d6bee104fa85821c9068ca731acc4342a06763d94434ca81aa92e6c118b7" :answer/id #uuid "71b97105-4333-5266-8968-c69b063df605" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115941/selection/6f507a253adbdcc4bedc6ea7f194a832c2f47c5ac10f63887f3e47550ceb5219" :answer/id #uuid "ccdd1474-0591-55f5-821c-feb3f22800b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115941/selection/ead0a4154493e524d04c53d4d32cf39752dbec66f3b275fd2e780db5faaf8939" :answer/id #uuid "1aab3742-50fc-5bf6-8c8d-55931897464d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=y(y+1)(2-y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115941/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a71dd1b3-b660-5833-8400-e07f3e2cdc68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115941/selection/c2aed1dd8c352973477ebb4eeeee8a60a835bc6afc8f05f5a6b1471f9ecded02" :answer-field/choices ["q-115941/selection/4a08887e14d194718cb72797be31ec63233d4df5b56923b20aae560d0fa5a147" "q-115941/selection/c2aed1dd8c352973477ebb4eeeee8a60a835bc6afc8f05f5a6b1471f9ecded02" "q-115941/selection/6c81d6bee104fa85821c9068ca731acc4342a06763d94434ca81aa92e6c118b7" "q-115941/selection/6f507a253adbdcc4bedc6ea7f194a832c2f47c5ac10f63887f3e47550ceb5219" "q-115941/selection/ead0a4154493e524d04c53d4d32cf39752dbec66f3b275fd2e780db5faaf8939"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115941" :question/id #uuid "8a58b057-32ea-5a42-a834-74970c5010b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-115941" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/9be53ddfc5bd7a471ced46b76f6c8bb78fbc8b6831cec1689b6d98e8561c0d87.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/50b53f35a936263f72a3962e32d841dc85519fb5ef3e7e138111c4efeb38137e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-2) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(y+1)(2-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{′}=y(y+1)(2-y).$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ it also evaluates to zero when $y=0,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-115941/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ca086fd-9732-5c4b-9cb0-168433d79203"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115941"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115940/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "1c0ee75c-43cd-56f1-bd82-42c28885096a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115940/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "1c6000b8-ce60-57f1-b233-3244b9f8994e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115940/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "65d43874-65f4-578d-9718-b5e6432b9757" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115940/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "077f0dac-5374-5a0b-aff6-e6d58343cb4f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115940/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "06215df3-fb34-51b4-87e2-a154e3b3abbe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115940/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "41c09ba2-9813-5158-a120-598a3b499931" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115940/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-115940/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-115940/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-115940/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-115940/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-115940/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115940" :question/id #uuid "8e8515e5-bf34-50ba-8279-46c17d9b91fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-115940" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/92c557774a625d93c34fea181f435c1e6e544daa606a76c512bd96a7b9c047e7.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(3-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-3)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-1)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/583fe0e13218462bdb4286ce456b0bcc4d43dd3772033e62a53242107a0a69db.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=0$ and $y=3.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=3$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<3$ | $y>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(3-y)$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-3)^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=3,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<3$ | $y>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-115940/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ca086fd-9732-5c4b-9cb0-168433d79203"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115940"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-116150/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "6514e6e8-9f9c-56a8-a1f3-a65db30f754e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116150/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "129b7255-a50c-507e-919f-2ee16efe774c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116150/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "e01615d7-3f77-5bc5-aa72-53fea87fc2a5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116150/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "53bd281b-bf8c-5d65-9736-ebf364853ef9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116150/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "7cd80012-7dc7-589b-a7aa-db23a84a638f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116150/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d04f0e4c-7667-5285-85f6-c703117462b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-116150/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-116150/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-116150/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-116150/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-116150/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-116150/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-116150" :question/id #uuid "31f2f4de-cad5-594e-ac30-aec769441ed5" :question/math-academy-id "q-116150" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/158e4f6067ce12d5a31cc6ee0b217a8d11a459db5883fdd0b28ffdc2bd969a5e.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}-2{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{3}+4{y}^{2}+4y$\n- ${y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/41b6dc7fd4e9a7dba6e14fe2d9fb87e7c07736ec0fc3cc111dd347b61c1075bb.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}-2{y}^{2}=-{y}^{2}(y+2)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+4{y}^{2}+4y=y({y}^{2}+4y+4)=y(y+2)^{2}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-2$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}=y(2-y)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-116150/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "800080f9-db79-5218-acaf-04040ab28a5d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-116150"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120177/selection/30a5cca05f45578ba3c8fdc58397d6f5eab3f791c55d3d46c923ad1f9088ad56" :answer/id #uuid "84b25da4-79ef-567c-8b20-b9b54ac9b3fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120177/selection/4bf18f8ca5b2a25140fab6f3a254508341ca708305d9d1d279976e568f85c2fe" :answer/id #uuid "09652e71-b6a0-5e93-9146-201a70956aac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2{y}^{2}+y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120177/selection/10b9e326f734d2a4c8daf4e4c445c3ccdd1217ca0ffecd3c7ed75a2ae73171aa" :answer/id #uuid "3caef30d-9f08-5bde-a094-d108cdbdf078" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120177/selection/61a64fe40520066f45055f56a5754d66c0771e295c9ac8d558df1b070c2f22d5" :answer/id #uuid "67a3767d-f6cb-5912-9a81-6889c464a976" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+2{y}^{2}+y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120177/selection/c92b9016743ac7fa9088b65649d0778bc953bacb0de7f5d867472a13af37aa56" :answer/id #uuid "cef94c6b-a8cb-53c7-b44c-018dea98c37d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120177/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a1e9355f-771c-56cf-aeab-9931dd38b955" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120177/selection/10b9e326f734d2a4c8daf4e4c445c3ccdd1217ca0ffecd3c7ed75a2ae73171aa" :answer-field/choices ["q-120177/selection/30a5cca05f45578ba3c8fdc58397d6f5eab3f791c55d3d46c923ad1f9088ad56" "q-120177/selection/4bf18f8ca5b2a25140fab6f3a254508341ca708305d9d1d279976e568f85c2fe" "q-120177/selection/10b9e326f734d2a4c8daf4e4c445c3ccdd1217ca0ffecd3c7ed75a2ae73171aa" "q-120177/selection/61a64fe40520066f45055f56a5754d66c0771e295c9ac8d558df1b070c2f22d5" "q-120177/selection/c92b9016743ac7fa9088b65649d0778bc953bacb0de7f5d867472a13af37aa56"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120177" :question/id #uuid "35f03faa-5461-5650-97e1-a210be6e8441" :question/math-academy-id "q-120177" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/8d004b698e288cbfa3cab67b1744bd9bf40c018267fa1f44a633019f6f047a9a.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/895353a5359d43f8f6256453d4f97a4f35b33acd1241f0a2950b9b21f6313f50.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}=2{y}^{2}(y-2)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4}={y}^{3}(2-y).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}=2{y}^{2}(y-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-120177/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "800080f9-db79-5218-acaf-04040ab28a5d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120177"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-116174/selection/d9a88339238e967eb9a15d45123d10d3302fca483e71d9878c1794df6a50e963" :answer/id #uuid "7c953b27-c5e7-5fcc-897c-388b13d33867" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=3y-{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116174/selection/ab8c02d36de05754ce82485df7cfb20a229722b8ea2e1664da41447cd38f8d26" :answer/id #uuid "7d6a98f0-ff1a-549c-bdc8-ba332d0a0a7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=3{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116174/selection/9e785e737f123f1871d24de79746c8124b57e7a4ea097199e05f6a1992147eaa" :answer/id #uuid "11b57f26-8990-5ca2-9cc0-995ccf3e08ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116174/selection/2dd2140f9c6b327fbb9db80fd5145d5c13376c40e9f1d79eaa7e6667e65caa3d" :answer/id #uuid "4c25163c-f30b-5e16-88c0-91b7e09eb3f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-9y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116174/selection/4ec2ee6118f0bf54adbcd0c103c54cc654a27c4afc61de02fee36cf72079cbec" :answer/id #uuid "60bc6cbb-a2ef-516a-8ca2-6ccd95cb5c08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-3y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116174/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7572c058-ad5a-5f9d-ad76-fe2d14b72320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-116174/selection/2dd2140f9c6b327fbb9db80fd5145d5c13376c40e9f1d79eaa7e6667e65caa3d" :answer-field/choices ["q-116174/selection/d9a88339238e967eb9a15d45123d10d3302fca483e71d9878c1794df6a50e963" "q-116174/selection/ab8c02d36de05754ce82485df7cfb20a229722b8ea2e1664da41447cd38f8d26" "q-116174/selection/9e785e737f123f1871d24de79746c8124b57e7a4ea097199e05f6a1992147eaa" "q-116174/selection/2dd2140f9c6b327fbb9db80fd5145d5c13376c40e9f1d79eaa7e6667e65caa3d" "q-116174/selection/4ec2ee6118f0bf54adbcd0c103c54cc654a27c4afc61de02fee36cf72079cbec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-116174" :question/id #uuid "b75bc494-1576-5354-8303-fbc7667847e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-116174" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/eeacce39a60a75fd71a77d54f0008f4469395d5e9b69912db8ce9896320b667f.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/dbb1d802854d4046479c69d297fe1807a9ded488409b385a6a2cffbf1196b4c4.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=3.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-9y=-y({y}^{2}-6y+9)=-y(y-3)^{2}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-3y=y(y-3).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=3$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<3$ | $y>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-9y=-y(y-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=-{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-9y.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-116174/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "800080f9-db79-5218-acaf-04040ab28a5d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-116174"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-116180/selection/e75a1afaa346c3f9867b897758125a59b6f4c2e871a435c646cf88802e11ab99" :answer/id #uuid "ebcca911-28ee-551b-a241-4543683394c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116180/selection/9dad16628bbb36409ead955beccdace3e9b06e1b0d56ee82e665a8f8a7cb3223" :answer/id #uuid "9d5127f1-fe51-5943-9856-7b1dd2716ba1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+4{y}^{2}+4y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116180/selection/fe9816195f75a6e46ac5d37280aade1fae679abd2e5aea547166dca10ce4011e" :answer/id #uuid "9d948788-8ca2-59d3-a4cf-35415dea2a4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=3{y}^{4}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116180/selection/59a6a976440d19e1d818c89fafc22693035f5fc97a2e6803827891fa553d5b59" :answer/id #uuid "cce3226c-452d-580a-9a03-de0b652a1869" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{4}+{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116180/selection/8871aa3578387d449dec9b68beecc59e8c651ee6e07ae9a9785961a32c49d6bd" :answer/id #uuid "43adfde6-2901-56c4-a0ad-7df55f56d1b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116180/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "90e75eb2-d763-5d94-9198-a9684c60f4fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-116180/selection/e75a1afaa346c3f9867b897758125a59b6f4c2e871a435c646cf88802e11ab99" :answer-field/choices ["q-116180/selection/e75a1afaa346c3f9867b897758125a59b6f4c2e871a435c646cf88802e11ab99" "q-116180/selection/9dad16628bbb36409ead955beccdace3e9b06e1b0d56ee82e665a8f8a7cb3223" "q-116180/selection/fe9816195f75a6e46ac5d37280aade1fae679abd2e5aea547166dca10ce4011e" "q-116180/selection/59a6a976440d19e1d818c89fafc22693035f5fc97a2e6803827891fa553d5b59" "q-116180/selection/8871aa3578387d449dec9b68beecc59e8c651ee6e07ae9a9785961a32c49d6bd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-116180" :question/id #uuid "db274ba7-dfb6-5f4f-a2db-8b2fa820f6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-116180" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/6d0a816057423c4f5397f876f775b0431eac550bb1a02d4e4f7021c33c37809e.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/f3b0dad3f1cb4b2216e3fce7f1fefa8beac15a6e16ab09ab3a4c47a2472e2f9a.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=-3$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}={y}^{3}(y+3)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}={y}^{2}(y+3).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-3$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-3$ | $-3<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}={y}^{3}(y+3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-116180/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "800080f9-db79-5218-acaf-04040ab28a5d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-116180"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120176/selection/2ef4ccd5f822f34ea55a0e03db7a283a5b2475a3966e6ef316b7aef74c007430" :answer/id #uuid "41dad35d-a04d-5fcb-9983-3fd4fa201eab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120176/selection/a10ca5832c97eb089c20ac616c5f868035310e0262ae23e371fa17ebe896e9cc" :answer/id #uuid "46ea7bde-54fb-53d9-94a0-2314d940ad54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}+2y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120176/selection/8be9be1a4e1edc6001473ce658d19826c28bf9159850532b41a654d5fb70eedd" :answer/id #uuid "a0215f4c-2fd6-556c-8c9b-52e5d10c12d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120176/selection/55c30fdfddae57e9f16e669baaff223e35dd69e122b4b939264d233d3436efeb" :answer/id #uuid "ee6e0427-dc0d-538b-b8ab-d8f4c2cf984c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=y+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120176/selection/714bf1c3331ed7cd3640adaff2f056565c80c8a0fe111e6397ef07bc3cb1bc52" :answer/id #uuid "eba50d76-d9f5-5089-842e-07d51f06590b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120176/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d376dcd8-6ab3-5e00-965c-4c71cc887b0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120176/selection/714bf1c3331ed7cd3640adaff2f056565c80c8a0fe111e6397ef07bc3cb1bc52" :answer-field/choices ["q-120176/selection/2ef4ccd5f822f34ea55a0e03db7a283a5b2475a3966e6ef316b7aef74c007430" "q-120176/selection/a10ca5832c97eb089c20ac616c5f868035310e0262ae23e371fa17ebe896e9cc" "q-120176/selection/8be9be1a4e1edc6001473ce658d19826c28bf9159850532b41a654d5fb70eedd" "q-120176/selection/55c30fdfddae57e9f16e669baaff223e35dd69e122b4b939264d233d3436efeb" "q-120176/selection/714bf1c3331ed7cd3640adaff2f056565c80c8a0fe111e6397ef07bc3cb1bc52"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120176" :question/id #uuid "d1906b3f-2cb7-58b7-9f03-df08a78bc003" :question/math-academy-id "q-120176" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/440d321dd0eb1b5687b392aac95d5d7d36e5a31d4cb95d5397f88afe229d7aa6.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/0a8b34730f001c218b6c380b9f5af0ff4b2ffd8fbb295f82e4961c5242051986.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y=y(y-2)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}={y}^{2}(2-y).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}={y}^{2}(2-y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120176/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "800080f9-db79-5218-acaf-04040ab28a5d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120176"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-116040/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "1e2520e5-bba1-591b-8b1e-d69400ed3874" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116040/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "9479bf72-de58-51a0-af9c-b6beed775887" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116040/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "a1aa5239-8a35-5cd1-99ef-772d9e0a00f7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116040/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "3f210340-3870-54dc-94c5-5a0a29010007" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116040/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "6dd18036-320b-5082-8f0f-9077e89d3c70" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116040/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a5c0ae67-1820-5b19-9dae-454cd1c16ee4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-116040/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-116040/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-116040/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-116040/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-116040/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-116040/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-116040" :question/id #uuid "b18f97d9-f92d-5fde-89dd-212b5f96a0c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-116040" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/ddb340a99b8ab92d60e17184274854ce3d1b1bfa4972a79e74b8bfe9b0a81ce7.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y+3}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+3)^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{y+3}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/73ba54766cd08a0b596e43cd8da93209e2548c68821e70a44418a24dfdf27a38.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to -3.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y<-3$ and negative for $y>-3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y+3}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to -3.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y<-3$ and negative for $y>-3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{(y+3)^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for all $y\\ne -3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{y+3}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{y}{y+3}=-\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{3}{y})}\\to -1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\infty .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-116040/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-116040"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-115479"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/1b608fabe99c6062f9d9f04cc7f3b2933e453812ed4ce9783908666c71c1c74b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/7bb52825944a7515a6281a88b5bc740cf6d097380fa13fe7e00efc5e499d49f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive everywhere.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{y-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y<0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 0.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for all $y\\ne 0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-116177/selection/b84b9a8e5e78dc571dcba4aa3a57d1b1e6f6b6f97093e04282fb61bda40c02f2" :answer/id #uuid "b3448238-3b73-56de-b9cd-7fccc0de471d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y-1)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116177/selection/867cfb0a87f595581224ecd6920be889c92b59a551c3f9340c237a3957826f25" :answer/id #uuid "08bfd063-6f2f-52b9-9627-c2d25cbfcdf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116177/selection/594596feea67c56926105ee2d6fdca4569bf04aa65331e0d54ae4cea6b747f88" :answer/id #uuid "dbfa02c7-833c-5efe-ba59-eb3b574ad385" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116177/selection/e03f67c685e300758e720667296883141d98bc053ef41111e8dfeb5cbcbaa0f8" :answer/id #uuid "16b9f17b-75de-540c-9834-565e7d0a0a0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{(y+1)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116177/selection/2300bcc987f980fe56efc950377117830527c03458c9cfb9275cc6c094476096" :answer/id #uuid "7d9044e2-97c2-5820-9088-65a28dff418a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116177/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd255b98-87ad-51ca-83be-5930aba26863" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-116177/selection/867cfb0a87f595581224ecd6920be889c92b59a551c3f9340c237a3957826f25" :answer-field/choices ["q-116177/selection/b84b9a8e5e78dc571dcba4aa3a57d1b1e6f6b6f97093e04282fb61bda40c02f2" "q-116177/selection/867cfb0a87f595581224ecd6920be889c92b59a551c3f9340c237a3957826f25" "q-116177/selection/594596feea67c56926105ee2d6fdca4569bf04aa65331e0d54ae4cea6b747f88" "q-116177/selection/e03f67c685e300758e720667296883141d98bc053ef41111e8dfeb5cbcbaa0f8" "q-116177/selection/2300bcc987f980fe56efc950377117830527c03458c9cfb9275cc6c094476096"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-116177" :question/id #uuid "d0cf6ca5-4b24-52a6-9510-ae0b22a2d68e" :question/math-academy-id "q-116177" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/8f023d50d11e5b6604e5fcc257e35aa4b8615e8c91f5855d10e73293720c27e4.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/b6cf036f732d1cdff6f2e575b2ce082979d986416afd195282789303dcf70131.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 1.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}$ become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}$ do *not* become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nSo, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)(y-1)^{2}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-116177/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-116177"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-116038/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "fb950dbe-9671-55d1-b138-caa905e0fcaa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116038/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "d41e9604-0d94-5e4c-b65c-63080f31c6f6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116038/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "9eb8be23-8455-52ab-868c-75a3ec81a649" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116038/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "d1ff1397-8951-572c-a3ae-31abc2d1a192" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116038/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "102a2155-0c38-564f-946d-62238737d750" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116038/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bbb1feca-29c3-579f-8d6c-bee8c2363c6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-116038/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-116038/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-116038/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-116038/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-116038/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-116038/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-116038" :question/id #uuid "53ab2db1-0308-5084-926c-c3a100e39237" :question/math-academy-id "q-116038" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/f7000c9b2360173996e462883bdf9f89af674b9f099f25a866cd95bd286d9150.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4-{y}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-4}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/5f87023b47e66436ca4f6caa8e8cc651e7de80ab09e346d955e1bb12eea211b6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 2.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )$ and positive for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4-{y}^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to \\pm 2.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty )$ and  positive for $y\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{4}{{y}^{2}}-1)}\\to -1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-4}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y\\in (-2,2)$ and positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-116038/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-116038"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-116179/selection/bf62186aa6fbb0808f13dbe54d81884283cd6ddfaf654ee991cebb11f8111c07" :answer/id #uuid "80497117-d1bc-5b5b-8215-5386df6cc0d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-2y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116179/selection/babc467007523d8575dbe779062d32116727dd34f8b8488667f5c9474a4830c7" :answer/id #uuid "adb0fa90-7eec-59c5-9b9e-f5011a5e0d95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y-2)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116179/selection/9dcaa0a03bd069c42918e362c70905cb3c2a9d4495349626752916d8d0b4b030" :answer/id #uuid "2bc2ff0c-d522-53cc-ab4e-3e9e1dafd6be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{(y+1)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116179/selection/c5eae71ad4f0a0994a7247291ed8ec011c9d95d5806d4c1ad0810bb8b1939dde" :answer/id #uuid "ea2ca5bb-bd98-5421-8dbd-c8d6b5431cf7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116179/selection/9688a4f181fe8a18bfe0656d8f08d0fac427343262da819d567a4441ef5ff2e2" :answer/id #uuid "d63798ff-0479-5d13-878a-735cc800f51c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116179/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4f547cd1-82bf-5b13-8e77-d0c062fd0344" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-116179/selection/c5eae71ad4f0a0994a7247291ed8ec011c9d95d5806d4c1ad0810bb8b1939dde" :answer-field/choices ["q-116179/selection/bf62186aa6fbb0808f13dbe54d81884283cd6ddfaf654ee991cebb11f8111c07" "q-116179/selection/babc467007523d8575dbe779062d32116727dd34f8b8488667f5c9474a4830c7" "q-116179/selection/9dcaa0a03bd069c42918e362c70905cb3c2a9d4495349626752916d8d0b4b030" "q-116179/selection/c5eae71ad4f0a0994a7247291ed8ec011c9d95d5806d4c1ad0810bb8b1939dde" "q-116179/selection/9688a4f181fe8a18bfe0656d8f08d0fac427343262da819d567a4441ef5ff2e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-116179" :question/id #uuid "65e3360c-8bc5-5de3-ad51-6c40acc5b036" :question/math-academy-id "q-116179" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/7b3aa65a62cb70ae297b57c5cfceecec8035afb621acec93d6fceabd1fd48729.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/890640809739a95a6d068f65678e124cc92d9e6d2fd86ec0380ee1f37a1c56d1.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to 2$ only. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2-y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{(y-2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the line $y=2$ divides the plane into $2$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $2$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2-y}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-116179/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-116179"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9306"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/8a4c773c2ccc0bbab1ebb7126b781504549a46b6f5991753866eff98f3c8e03d.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = y^2(y-1)$\n\n- $y' = y(y-1)(y-4)$\n\n- $y' = -(y-1)^2(y-4)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/ee21aabb9f81aed281951e5ff9420c5f2985bfbc192791d8d2bbd1f1c87de156.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y = 1$ and $y = 4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation $y' = y^2(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=1.$\n\n- The equation $y' = -y(y-1)(y-4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=0,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation $y' =-(y-1)^2(y-4)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9307"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/998b30d8fb1df0c32a6ec63881ea247702a8e3888e7cd63ca371bc7dfe40ee2d.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- $y = -y^2(y+4)$\n\n- $y = y(y-4)^2$\n\n- $y = y(y+4)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/fb7c6c77767368871d3b39991093906f6a4a0f94e2a83075570e22b3f0e87a78.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-{y}^{2}(y+4) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(y+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y <                                    -4$ | $-4 <                                    y <                                    0$ | $y > 0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\ny' = -y^2(y+4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y' = -y^2(y+4).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9314"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/b064d9170c89eda881a1247b14fa295bae6b78d794ad7737aa86872fb5277af1.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- $y' = y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y$\n\n- $y' = y^2 - 2y$\n\n- $y' = y^3 + y^2$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/96fa01db29c0effec6c81996cfcaad1d46ffa71f2f1b2b3da0a48dc39bfb0d26.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\ny'= y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y = y(y^2 - 4y + 4) = y(y-2)^2\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\ny'=  y^2 - 2y = y(y-2).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y <        0$ | $0 <          y <          2$ | $y > 2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\searrow$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\ny ' = y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y =y(y-2)^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y' =y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9308"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/f93764bdd958d41e90e09e8be2f52754835c67bc5a2ff9f6186454ebbd245232.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{y}{y+1}$\n\n- $y'= \\dfrac1{y^2+y}$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{2}{y+1}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/8820a0d0a4e0fc9d0089dea3c34a67bfaf619edcb7e0552d14a11b42a4cd20e5.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to -1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y > -1$ and negative for $y < -1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{y}{y+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y}{y+1} = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(1+\\dfrac1y\\right)}\\to 1 \\quad\\text{as} \\quad y\\to \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac1{y^2+y}= \\dfrac1{y(y+1)}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become vertical for *both* $y \\to -1$ *and* $y \\to 0,$ \nwhich is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{2}{y+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that $y'\\to 0$ as $y\\to\\pm\\infty,$ and $y'\\to \\infty$ as $y\\to -1.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y > -1$ and negative for $y < -1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
